               <history>
                  <origin>
                  <p>Written in <origPlace>Norway</origPlace>, probably in the Bergen area, <origDate notBefore="1260" notAfter="1290">c. 1270</origDate>.</p>
                  <p>DG 4-7 now consists of 42 leaves in seven gatherings, each of originally 8
                     leaves. Thus, the complete manuscript once had (7 x 8) = 56 leaves. The first
                     gathering lacks the middle pair and another leaf, which has been cut out. The
                     second gathering also lacks the middle pair. The third and fourth gatherings
                     are complete. The fifth gathering lacks the middle pair, and the sixth
                     gathering lacks the fourth leaf, which has been cut out. The seventh gathering
                     only has the outer pair of leaves. Remnants of the seventh gathering were found
                     as the lining of a bishop’s mitra in Skálholt, and are now catalouged as AM
                     666b 4to in Copenhagen.</p>
                  <p>At the very beginning of DG 4-7, there are two leaves containing a fragment of
                     "Óláfs saga Tryggvasonar". In the present state of the manuscript (and in the
                     facsimile edition by Mattias Tveitane, 1972), they are fols. 1–2.</p>
                  <p>The main part of the manuscript are fols. 3–44, containing the initial two
                     thirds of "Pamphilus saga", the concluding 13 lines of a dialogue between
                     courage and fear, "Elis saga" and finally "Strengleikar". Note that in the
                     facsimile edition and also in the present digital edition, the leaves have been
                     numbered consecutively in the order they now have in the manuscript,
                     irrespective of lost leaves and leaves which have been put in the wrong order
                     as a result of the later binding of the book.</p>
                  <p>The information compiled here is based on the introduction in the facsimile
                     edition by Mattias Tveitane (1972).</p>
                  </origin>
               </history>
            </msDesc>
